gm-method (a 'deployed page has none of our updates' report when the deploy is actually GREEN+current): before assuming a stale/failed deploy, witness BOTH (a) deploy currency AND (b) the URL's own param-resolution path. A real case: the user's URL named a world (?world=X) that had been DELETED months earlier; a client-side redirect guard (KNOWN=[...] allowlist in the entry HTML) silently rewrote the unknown world to the default, so the user was viewing the default world -- which DID carry every update -- while believing it was world X. The 'missing updates' was a phantom: they were loading a non-existent world. Diagnose: (1) gh run list shows green + latest commit deployed; (2) live browser witness of the EXACT user URL shows location.search rewritten to the default world (the redirect fired) and booted=true/pageErrors=[]; (3) git log -S/--diff-filter=D confirms the named world file was intentionally removed. Also: when a repo runs a push-triggered declaudeify/filter-branch force-push CI concurrently with the deploy, note that such a rewrite mutates only author/committer METADATA + strips trailers -- it never changes file CONTENT, so a deploy-vs-rewrite race cannot stale the deployed page's content (the deploy ships working-tree files, identical pre/post rewrite). The benign-content insight stops you from 'fixing' an intentional CI. Lesson: green+content-current+correct-redirect = the page is fine; the bug is in the user's URL/expectation, surface that via a question rather than churning code.